# s2u-yt — managed yt-dlp config (installed into ~/.local/share/s2u-yt/conf/config).
#
# This file is applied AFTER your ~/.config/yt-dlp/config via --config-locations,
# so anything you already have (cookies, js runtimes, ...) keeps working and this
# file wins on any conflict.
#
# Client strategy — android_vr first, web_safari fallback:
#
# * android_vr (anonymous): the only client on this bot-checked IP whose
#   googlevideo URLs are not rejected (verified live: HTTP 200/206). It serves
#   DASH video up to 2160p (vs web_safari's 1080p HLS ceiling) and single-file
#   audio (opus/m4a) that MPD can range-seek — fixing stream seekability too.
#   yt-dlp only runs it when the session has NO cookies (SUPPORTS_COOKIES is
#   false), so the wrapper strips the user config's cookie options for the
#   first attempt and retries with them if it fails.
#
# * web_safari (authenticated fallback): the previously verified-good path
#   (HLS up to 1080p). When the anonymous pass fails (or a video needs the
#   session), the wrapper retries with your cookies — yt-dlp then auto-skips
#   android_vr (cookies present) and uses web_safari.
#
# PO tokens: android_vr's GVS policy requires them; the bgutil provider mints
# them (systemd --user unit s2u-yt-bgutil.service). If bgutil is down the
# anonymous pass degrades to low-quality formats — keep it running (status.sh
# checks it). If YouTube ever extends the SABR experiment to android_vr, the
# web_safari fallback keeps playback working at 1080p.
--extractor-args "youtube:player_client=android_vr,web_safari"
